Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Roof Refractor Scrapes Orion
- Story & Context: Deep in the flooded No. 4 shaft outside Hazard, Kentucky, night-shift electrician Harlen Cobb wired a CB radio directly into a section of pressurized copper pipe, inadvertently broadcasting a haunting harmonica melody across local emergency bands. The acoustic resonance of the subterranean conduit created a staggering three-second natural decay that caught the ear of local amateur astronomers monitoring low-frequency radio waves from a nearby flat-top roof. The resulting recording became the underground tape-trade sensation that birthed the scene, launching a movement that treated underground pipe-reverb as an outer-space tracking tool. "We pulled the light straight through two miles of shale," Harlen rasped into his headset as the brass section began to swell around his instrument.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark album art showing a crude brass telescope pointed out of a cracked concrete skylight toward the constellation Orion, with glowing red underground mine pipes coiling around the tripod on a misty dark night.

Track 2: Aperture-12 (Rust on the Primary Mirror)
- Story & Context: Recorded live in an abandoned observatory turned squat-venue, this track centers on former drill operator Maeve Cross confessing her fear of open sky after a decade working beneath the dirt. During the performance, she held a single high-G note on her blues harp for two full minutes while the string section built a terrifying wall of orchestral noise behind her. The performance was nearly halted when the vibrations loosened decades of rust from the observatory's iron dome, showering the audience in fine red dust. "Don't leave me out under all that open blue," she moaned into the microphone, her voice echoing into a cavernous sub-bass drop.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Cinematic lyric background featuring a close-up of an old telescope lens covered in flakes of red iron rust, reflecting a cold starry sky framed by dark jagged coal rocks.

Track 3: Did You Measure the Parallax, Jesse?
- Story & Context: The EP concludes with the urban legend of Jesse Thorne, a miner who vanished into an unmapped drift after claiming he could pinpoint stargazing coordinates using the acoustic ping of ventilation ducting. Search parties found only his brass-plated harmonica and a series of complex celestial calculations scratched onto a steel air vent with a drill bit. The track weaves actual structural pipe-tap recordings from the rescue effort into an epic, slow-burning orchestral finale featuring an uncredited thirty-piece string section. "I found the angle where the darkness ends," echoes through a fog of pitch-shifted brass and thunderous percussion.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Poetic image of a solitary miner's helmet laying on a dirt cavern floor, its headlamp beam shooting straight up through a narrow shaft to illuminate a tiny cluster of distant stars.

